=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The helpdesk ticket claims that leaf_router_0_1 (the top-of-rack switch serving
pc_0_1's rack) has lost both of its uplinks to the spine layer and is therefore
isolated. A full diagnostic sweep of the live lab disproves this assertion on
every measured dimension.

Both uplink interfaces on leaf_router_0_1 are administratively and operationally
UP: eth0 (172.16.0.7/31, peering with spine_router_0_0) and eth1
(172.16.0.11/31, peering with spine_router_0_1) show no errors, no drops, and
active RX/TX counters. The BGP sessions on both uplinks are in the Established
state and are exchanging prefixes normally (PfxRcd = 1 from each spine). The
routing table on leaf_router_0_1 carries a live BGP-learned route to 10.0.0.0/24
via 172.16.0.6 (spine_router_0_0), and leaf_router_0_0 symmetrically holds a
route to 10.0.1.0/24 via 172.16.0.4. Both spine routers and the super-spine
have correct forwarding entries for both host subnets. An ICMP test from pc_0_0
(10.0.0.2) to pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) returns 4/4 packets with < 2 ms RTT and zero
loss.

Root cause: There is no fault. The fabric is operating exactly as designed.
The information relayed to the user by "infra folks" is incorrect; no link
failure, BGP session drop, or routing black-hole exists anywhere in the
topology. No corrective action is required.
=== END ===
